Classification and Regression Trees (CART)
This directory contains practical implementations and exercises completed as part of Chapter 1 of the Machine Learning with Tree-Based Models in Python course.

📌 Overview & Core Concepts
Supervised learning models known as Classification and Regression Trees (CART) split the feature space recursively to make predictions:

Classification Trees: Used to predict categorical target labels by dividing feature regions. Splitting criteria can be evaluated using either the Gini Index or Entropy (Information Gain).

Regression Trees: Used to predict continuous numeric targets by minimizing variance or Mean Squared Error (MSE) within terminal leaves.

🛠️ Summary of Exercises & Code Implementations
1. Training & Evaluating a Classification Tree
Dataset: Wisconsin Breast Cancer Dataset (sklearn.datasets)

Task: Predict whether a tumor is malignant or benign based on radius_mean and concave points_mean.

Implementation:

Python
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score

# Instantiate and fit the classifier
dt = DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=6, random_state=1)
dt.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Predict and evaluate accuracy
y_pred = dt.predict(X_test)
acc = accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)
print(f"Test set accuracy: {acc:.2f}")
2. Information Criteria: Entropy vs. Gini Index
Task: Trained a classification tree using all 30 features with criterion='entropy' and compared it against the Gini index criterion.

Key Takeaway: Both entropy and Gini index yield extremely high performance, though Gini index is often chosen by default due to faster computation speeds.

3. Training & Evaluating a Regression Tree
Dataset: Auto-MPG dataset

Task: Predict car fuel consumption (mpg) using available features.

Implementation:

Python
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor
from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error as MSE

# Instantiate and fit the regressor
dt_reg = DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=8, min_samples_leaf=0.13, random_state=3)
dt_reg.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Evaluate using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
y_pred_dt = dt_reg.predict(X_test)
rmse_dt = MSE(y_test, y_pred_dt) ** (1/2)
print(f"Regression Tree Test Set RMSE: {rmse_dt:.2f}")
4. Decision Trees vs. Linear Models
Compared the rectangular decision boundaries of decision trees against the single linear decision boundaries of Logistic Regression and Linear Regression.

Finding: Non-parametric tree models often capture non-linear interactions better, resulting in lower test errors on complex structured relationships.
